Look, all I&#39;m saying is that for all of these One-Shots, I run several test games first, getting used to how the heroes interact with the villain, the environment, and each other; keeping track of the major pitfalls to be wary of; and the like. &nbsp;(In this particular game, I played until I beat Dawn 3 times in a row, at which point I figured it was time to try the actual scenario.) &nbsp;When I get to a point that I&#39;m pretty comfortable that I have a good strategy to beat it, I play the One-Shot for real....

&nbsp;

....and invariably come across some strange combination of cards/bad draws that never came up (or even close to came up) in my practice games, which (a) give all of the heroes awful draws; (b) give the villains completely insane/impractical draws; (c) completely neuter one of the heroes; (d) disincentivize otherwise completely sensible plays that most people would make (like in this game, getting out Flak Cannon/Razor Ordinance) and/or (e) render all of the strategies and gambits from the practice games, or other &quot;conventional&quot; plays, completely useless. &nbsp;

&nbsp;

 So I play the scenario over and over and over again, losing and losing and losing again, trying different things until I happen on the *one* combination of plays/moves that manages to beat it. &nbsp; And quite often it is *one* combination, there&#39;s no room for error; playing *one* card differently makes the difference between winning and losing. &nbsp; And I think, &quot;Well, it wasn&#39;t *un*beatable,&quot; but also &quot;But how in the heck was I supposed to stumble upon that particular combination of moves/plays on the very first try, with no &#39;undo&#39;s?&quot; &nbsp;&nbsp; 

&nbsp;

 Then I read stuff on the boards along the lines of, &quot;Oh, yeah - I Minted by skipping all of the heroes&#39; first two turns...,&quot; or &quot;I Minted by having Argent Adept be my main damage dealer,&quot; or &quot;I Minted by using nothing but Wrathful Gaze...,&quot; or other moves that defy convention. &nbsp;My reactions tend to be along the lines of: &nbsp;(1) &quot;So...beating this setup requires doing something out of the ordinary.&quot; &nbsp;(2) &quot;But how do you know you have to play out-of-the ordinary without playing it and losing, therefore *not* getting Mint?&quot; &nbsp;&nbsp; 
<div>&nbsp;</div>
 Is the whole point of these things just to assume that the strategies one would normally employ won&#39;t work, and to start every game by experimenting with something bizarre, hoping that first time you&#39;ll luck upon the unusual combination that will get past the bad draws/bad deck lineup? 

&nbsp;

